1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,141
Every time you spend one hundred dollars
with a seller on Amazon...

2
00:00:05,181 --> 00:00:08,719
about fifty-two of those dollars never
reach the seller.

3
00:00:08,759 --> 00:00:09,614
Not the brand.

4
00:00:09,654 --> 00:00:10,636
Not the factory.

5
00:00:10,676 --> 00:00:11,084
Amazon.

6
00:00:11,124 --> 00:00:15,173
Last year, a single line in Amazon's
annual report — advertising

7
00:00:15,213 --> 00:00:18,432
— brought in sixty-eight point six billion
dollars.

8
00:00:18,472 --> 00:00:22,394
That's roughly one hundred and eighty-
eight million dollars...

9
00:00:22,434 --> 00:00:23,480
every single day.

10
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:27,761
In the next nine minutes, we'll follow one
hundred dollars of sales

11
00:00:27,801 --> 00:00:32,362
— dollar by dollar — through every fee,
every auction, and every return.

12
00:00:32,402 --> 00:00:34,854
And by the end, you'll see why the U.S.

13
00:00:34,894 --> 00:00:38,368
government has taken Amazon to court over
it — twice...

14
00:00:38,408 --> 00:00:43,671
and why, according to the FTC, you may be
paying this tax even if you never shop on

15
00:00:43,711 --> 00:00:44,566
Amazon at all.

16
00:00:44,956 --> 00:00:47,615
Here's a typical apparel seller on Amazon

17
00:00:47,655 --> 00:00:52,024
— modeled from Marketplace Pulse data and
Amazon's own fee schedule

18
00:00:52,064 --> 00:00:54,065
— per hundred dollars in sales.

19
00:00:54,105 --> 00:00:55,776
First, the product itself.

20
00:00:55,816 --> 00:00:58,935
Manufacturing, ocean freight, and import
duties.

21
00:00:58,975 --> 00:01:02,687
Twenty-eight dollars — paid months before
anything sells.

22
00:01:03,077 --> 00:01:05,861
The moment a customer clicks "Buy Now"...

23
00:01:05,901 --> 00:01:07,721
Amazon takes its first cut.

24
00:01:07,761 --> 00:01:10,821
A seventeen percent referral fee on
clothing.

25
00:01:10,861 --> 00:01:12,061
Seventeen dollars.

26
00:01:12,451 --> 00:01:14,279
Next, Fulfillment by Amazon.

27
00:01:14,319 --> 00:01:16,815
Picking, packing, shipping, storage...

28
00:01:16,855 --> 00:01:19,351
plus a new surcharge added this April.

29
00:01:19,391 --> 00:01:20,752
About twenty dollars.

30
00:01:21,142 --> 00:01:23,317
Then, the line shoppers never see.

31
00:01:23,357 --> 00:01:24,099
Advertising.

32
00:01:24,139 --> 00:01:26,314
Just to show up where buyers look,

33
00:01:26,354 --> 00:01:30,809
a typical seller spends around fifteen
dollars on sponsored listings.

34
00:01:31,199 --> 00:01:33,570
Returns and write-offs take another ten.

35
00:01:33,959 --> 00:01:34,903
The final split?

36
00:01:34,943 --> 00:01:39,882
The seller — who designed the product,
paid the factory, and carried all the risk

37
00:01:39,922 --> 00:01:41,481
— keeps about ten dollars.

38
00:01:41,521 --> 00:01:45,600
Amazon — without owning a single thread —
collects about fifty-two.

39
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:48,243
More than five times what the seller
keeps.

40
00:01:48,283 --> 00:01:50,210
And that fifteen-dollar ad line?

41
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:54,698
That's the one that quietly became a
sixty-eight-billion-dollar business.

42
00:01:56,688 --> 00:02:00,241
How did an online bookstore become a toll
collector?

43
00:02:00,281 --> 00:02:03,558
The answer is buried in Amazon's SEC Form
ten-K,

44
00:02:03,598 --> 00:02:07,151
under a line called "advertising
services." In 2025,

45
00:02:07,191 --> 00:02:10,675
it brought in sixty-eight point six
billion dollars

46
00:02:10,715 --> 00:02:13,508
— up twenty-two percent in a single year.

47
00:02:13,548 --> 00:02:14,751
For perspective...

48
00:02:14,791 --> 00:02:20,348
that's more than half the combined revenue
of every daily and weekly newspaper on

49
00:02:20,388 --> 00:02:20,763
Earth.

50
00:02:20,803 --> 00:02:25,669
Not all of it comes from sellers — Prime
Video and Twitch ads count too

51
00:02:25,709 --> 00:02:30,989
— but sponsored search listings are widely
estimated to be the biggest piece.

52
00:02:31,379 --> 00:02:32,628
Here's how it works.

53
00:02:32,668 --> 00:02:37,783
Depending on the survey, between half and
two-thirds of American online shoppers

54
00:02:37,823 --> 00:02:41,134
now start their product searches directly
on Amazon.

55
00:02:41,174 --> 00:02:43,840
And once the buyers are all in one room...

56
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:46,998
whoever controls the shelf controls the
business.

57
00:02:47,387 --> 00:02:50,025
So Amazon turned the shelf into an
auction.

58
00:02:50,065 --> 00:02:53,761
Try it yourself: search for almost
anything on your phone...

59
00:02:53,801 --> 00:02:57,871
and count the "Sponsored" results before
the first one that isn't.

60
00:02:57,910 --> 00:03:01,918
Every slot is a seller bidding, per click,
against other sellers.

61
00:03:02,308 --> 00:03:07,492
And according to a new lawsuit, even the
auction may not be what it seems.

62
00:03:07,532 --> 00:03:11,587
In August 2026, the FTC and twenty-two
states sued Amazon,

63
00:03:11,627 --> 00:03:17,234
alleging that since 2019 it secretly added
a surcharge on top of auction prices.

64
00:03:17,274 --> 00:03:23,023
Documents unsealed in November allege prices
up to fifty percent above the auction

65
00:03:23,063 --> 00:03:24,859
result on ordinary days...

66
00:03:24,899 --> 00:03:29,306
and up to two hundred and fifty percent on
days like Prime Day.

67
00:03:29,346 --> 00:03:34,248
The FTC puts the total overcharge at more
than twenty billion dollars.

68
00:03:34,288 --> 00:03:36,507
Amazon calls the suit misguided,

69
00:03:36,547 --> 00:03:41,025
and says it never charges more than an
advertiser's maximum bid.

70
00:03:41,415 --> 00:03:45,391
For sellers, it's a prisoner's dilemma:
bid away your margin...

71
00:03:45,431 --> 00:03:46,219
or disappear.

72
00:03:46,259 --> 00:03:49,470
So why don't they just charge less
everywhere else?

73
00:03:49,510 --> 00:03:50,873
Because if they try...

74
00:03:50,913 --> 00:03:52,020
a bot is watching.

75
00:03:54,010 --> 00:03:58,961
In 2023, the Federal Trade Commission and
a group of states sued Amazon for

76
00:03:59,001 --> 00:04:01,157
illegally maintaining a monopoly.

77
00:04:01,197 --> 00:04:06,214
The complaint describes what happens when
a seller tries to undercut Amazon.

78
00:04:06,254 --> 00:04:10,007
Say you sell a dress for one hundred
dollars on Amazon...

79
00:04:10,047 --> 00:04:14,599
and eighty-five on your own Shopify store,
where your fees are lower.

80
00:04:14,989 --> 00:04:20,129
According to the FTC, Amazon's algorithms
scan the internet for lower prices.

81
00:04:20,169 --> 00:04:23,425
If they find one, your offer can lose the
Buy Box

82
00:04:23,465 --> 00:04:25,780
— that yellow "Add to Cart" button.

83
00:04:25,820 --> 00:04:28,874
Industry estimates — never confirmed by
Amazon

84
00:04:28,914 --> 00:04:32,507
— say more than eighty percent of sales go
through it.

85
00:04:32,547 --> 00:04:34,323
The complaint goes further.

86
00:04:34,363 --> 00:04:39,907
It says Amazon can bury discounting sellers so
far down in search results that they

87
00:04:39,947 --> 00:04:44,953
become — quote — "effectively invisible."
So sellers do the rational thing.

88
00:04:44,993 --> 00:04:49,998
In the FTC's words, they use their
inflated Amazon prices as "a price floor

89
00:04:50,038 --> 00:04:51,276
everywhere else"...

90
00:04:51,316 --> 00:04:53,967
which means, if the government is right,

91
00:04:54,007 --> 00:04:56,927
you pay the Amazon tax even at other
stores.

92
00:04:57,317 --> 00:05:02,689
Then there's Project Nessie — an algorithm
the FTC says predicted when competitors

93
00:05:02,729 --> 00:05:05,263
would match Amazon's price increases...

94
00:05:05,303 --> 00:05:08,761
so Amazon could raise prices and watch
rivals follow.

95
00:05:08,801 --> 00:05:13,909
The agency alleges it generated more than
one billion dollars in extra profit.

96
00:05:13,949 --> 00:05:17,538
Amazon says the FTC "grossly
mischaracterizes" the tool

97
00:05:17,578 --> 00:05:21,630
— and that it was scrapped because it
didn't work as intended.

98
00:05:21,670 --> 00:05:26,382
More broadly, Amazon says the lawsuit is
wrong on the facts and the law.

99
00:05:26,422 --> 00:05:29,418
But there's one cost we haven't counted
yet...

100
00:05:29,458 --> 00:05:32,322
the one that arrives after the sale is
over.

101
00:05:34,312 --> 00:05:37,048
Now for the cost that comes back in a box.

102
00:05:37,088 --> 00:05:39,956
According to the National Retail
Federation,

103
00:05:39,996 --> 00:05:43,393
roughly one in five online purchases gets
sent back.

104
00:05:43,433 --> 00:05:47,953
In fashion, there's a name for one of the
most common forms of abuse.

105
00:05:47,993 --> 00:05:48,680
Wardrobing.

106
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:50,729
Buy an evening dress on Friday.

107
00:05:50,769 --> 00:05:52,910
Wear it to a wedding on Saturday.

108
00:05:52,950 --> 00:05:56,810
Return it on Monday — "didn't fit." In one
industry survey,

109
00:05:56,850 --> 00:06:00,908
sixty percent of retail executives said
they'd seen wardrobing

110
00:06:00,948 --> 00:06:04,278
— the most common type of return fraud on
the list.

111
00:06:04,318 --> 00:06:06,658
For the shopper, the return is free.

112
00:06:06,698 --> 00:06:11,813
For the seller, here's what one worn, one-
hundred-dollar dress actually costs.

113
00:06:12,203 --> 00:06:15,216
The customer gets their hundred dollars
back.

114
00:06:15,256 --> 00:06:18,066
Amazon refunds most of its referral fee...

115
00:06:18,106 --> 00:06:20,644
but keeps a refund administration fee.

116
00:06:20,684 --> 00:06:22,680
Three dollars and forty cents.

117
00:06:23,070 --> 00:06:26,832
The fulfillment fee Amazon charged to ship
the dress?

118
00:06:26,872 --> 00:06:27,765
Not refunded.

119
00:06:27,805 --> 00:06:29,199
About six dollars...

120
00:06:29,239 --> 00:06:29,558
gone.

121
00:06:29,948 --> 00:06:31,822
Then, a returns processing fee.

122
00:06:31,862 --> 00:06:33,428
Around three dollars more.

123
00:06:33,818 --> 00:06:38,936
And the dress itself comes back with makeup on
the collar and perfume in the fabric.

124
00:06:38,976 --> 00:06:40,410
It can't be sold as new.

125
00:06:40,450 --> 00:06:42,620
Twenty-eight dollars of inventory...

126
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:43,357
written off.

127
00:06:43,747 --> 00:06:47,174
Total: a loss of about forty dollars and
forty cents

128
00:06:47,214 --> 00:06:49,440
— on a sale that no longer exists.

129
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:53,174
At a ten percent margin, that's four full-
price sales...

130
00:06:53,214 --> 00:06:55,574
just to pay for one wardrobed dress.

131
00:06:55,614 --> 00:07:00,840
And even the sellers who survive returns
run into a trap that has nothing to do

132
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:01,840
with customers.

133
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:02,907
It's about time.

134
00:07:04,897 --> 00:07:07,123
Selling on Amazon starts with a bet.

135
00:07:07,163 --> 00:07:09,388
Sellers pay their factories up front

136
00:07:09,428 --> 00:07:13,668
— often two or three months before a
single unit reaches a customer.

137
00:07:14,057 --> 00:07:18,354
Then, when the sale finally happens, the
money still doesn't arrive.

138
00:07:18,394 --> 00:07:23,456
Under Amazon's current rules, seller funds
generally become available seven days

139
00:07:23,496 --> 00:07:26,007
after the customer's confirmed delivery.

140
00:07:26,047 --> 00:07:30,981
Add settlement cycles on top, and many
sellers wait two to four weeks to touch

141
00:07:31,021 --> 00:07:32,129
their own revenue.

142
00:07:32,519 --> 00:07:36,899
Meanwhile, the fees around the inventory
keep multiplying.

143
00:07:36,939 --> 00:07:42,233
Since 2024, Amazon has charged an inbound
placement fee — essentially,

144
00:07:42,273 --> 00:07:47,414
a fee based on how your boxes get
distributed across its warehouses.

145
00:07:47,454 --> 00:07:52,977
And in April 2026, it added a three-and-a-
half percent surcharge on every

146
00:07:53,017 --> 00:07:54,196
fulfillment fee.

147
00:07:54,587 --> 00:07:56,629
And then there's the corridor.

148
00:07:56,669 --> 00:08:01,625
Keep too much stock for too long, and you
pay aged inventory surcharges.

149
00:08:01,665 --> 00:08:06,344
But let your stock fall below roughly
twenty-eight days of supply...

150
00:08:06,384 --> 00:08:10,508
and you pay a low-inventory-level fee on
every unit shipped.

151
00:08:10,548 --> 00:08:11,965
Too much costs money.

152
00:08:12,005 --> 00:08:13,561
Too little costs money.

153
00:08:13,601 --> 00:08:15,643
Amazon collects on both sides.

154
00:08:15,683 --> 00:08:20,154
No surprise, then, that in Marketplace
Pulse's 2026 Seller Index,

155
00:08:20,194 --> 00:08:25,150
forty-nine percent of sellers named fees
as their biggest margin concern

156
00:08:25,190 --> 00:08:28,065
— and forty-six percent named advertising.

157
00:08:28,105 --> 00:08:28,412
So...

158
00:08:28,452 --> 00:08:29,869
why does anyone stay?

159
00:08:31,859 --> 00:08:35,960
On your own website, the payment processor
takes about three dollars on a

160
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:37,094
hundred-dollar sale.

161
00:08:37,134 --> 00:08:38,456
Sounds like a bargain...

162
00:08:38,496 --> 00:08:41,348
until you realize the customers don't come
with it.

163
00:08:41,388 --> 00:08:45,432
You have to buy them — one click at a time
— on Google, Meta, or TikTok.

164
00:08:45,472 --> 00:08:46,680
A different auction...

165
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:48,382
with the same tollbooth logic.

166
00:08:48,422 --> 00:08:50,594
And Amazon does deliver something real.

167
00:08:50,634 --> 00:08:53,657
Over two hundred million Prime members,
fast delivery,

168
00:08:53,697 --> 00:08:56,607
and a checkout button people trust without
thinking.

169
00:08:56,647 --> 00:08:58,592
That's what sellers are paying for.

170
00:08:58,982 --> 00:09:00,970
What's changed is the price.

171
00:09:01,010 --> 00:09:05,461
In 2016, Amazon's cut was about a third of
a seller's revenue.

172
00:09:05,501 --> 00:09:07,707
By 2022, it was more than half.

173
00:09:07,747 --> 00:09:13,502
And in 2027, a federal judge in Seattle
will start deciding whether parts of how

174
00:09:13,542 --> 00:09:15,675
Amazon got here broke the law.

175
00:09:15,715 --> 00:09:19,297
If the FTC wins, the Buy Box rules could
change...

176
00:09:19,337 --> 00:09:22,339
and with them, prices across the internet.

177
00:09:22,379 --> 00:09:24,947
If Amazon wins, the tollbooth stays.

178
00:09:25,337 --> 00:09:25,586
So...

179
00:09:25,626 --> 00:09:27,034
back to where we started.

180
00:09:27,074 --> 00:09:30,392
When you spend one hundred dollars with a
seller on Amazon

181
00:09:30,432 --> 00:09:32,187
— who actually gets your money?

182
00:09:32,227 --> 00:09:35,719
About ten dollars goes to the business
that made the product.

183
00:09:35,759 --> 00:09:38,961
About fifty-two goes to the company that
owns the shelf.

184
00:09:39,001 --> 00:09:43,130
In modern commerce, the company that makes
the product rarely gets rich.

185
00:09:43,170 --> 00:09:47,704
The fortune belongs to whoever controls
the last three seconds before checkout.

186
00:09:47,744 --> 00:09:51,004
Every number in this video is sourced in
the description.
